They are no longer byte-identical to upstream, in twelve ways.

  1. Only a subset ships. This plugin carried 45 of their region files; as of
     2026-08-24 it carries 27, covering nine places. The other eighteen files —
     4922, 9016, 9272, 9273, 9776, 10038, 10549, 11062, 11318, 11569, 11573,
     11577, 11826, 13110, 13622, 13878, 14131 and 14936 — were deleted outright,
     taking exactly thirty of their placements with them (26 citizens, 4 scenery).
     This was an editorial decision about what makes a finished plugin rather than
     any judgement about the data: thirteen of the twenty-four places held one or
     two figures each — ten of them held exactly one — and one figure in a town
     reads as a broken plugin rather than as ambient life.

     That accounts for thirteen of the fifteen. The rule applied was conjunctive —
     a real city with at least three figures — and the other two failed its first
     half rather than its second. Barrows (six figures, 14131.json) is a burial
     mound, and Piscatoris (eight, across 9016, 9272 and 9273) is a fishing colony
     and hunter area; both cleared the three-figure bar, and neither is a place a
     player would call a city. The rule as the code states it is in
     src/main/java/com/matthewmariner/livelycities/City.java.

     Shipping a subset is itself a modification and is stated here for that
     reason. BSD-2 does not require redistributing a work whole, but it does
     require that nobody reading this assumes the 27 files are the whole of what
     they published.

     Two of the modifications this notice used to disclose went with those files
     and are therefore no longer claimed anywhere: the six citizens in 14131.json
     that had been renamed from the Barrows Brothers to generic "Barrow wight"s,
     and one of the corrected "moveAnimation" records listed at item 4 ("Draug",
     14936.json). Neither file ships, so neither change is in anything a licensee
     receives from this project.

